          "text": "{\"category\":\"suspected_DAQ_transient_or_auto_restart\",\"cause\":\"unknown\",\"action\":\"Check DAQ and operating-system logs at the 35 anomalous subruns for process restarts, file boundaries, CPU saturation, dropped events, synchronization loss, or queue buildup. If a DAQ process stall or restart is confirmed, restart MilliDAQ cleanly and verify configuration consistency; do not alter LVDS or PMT hardware without corroborating evidence.\",\"confidence\":0.62,\"supporting_historical_runs\":[1604,1500,1605],\"reasoning_summary\":\"OBSERVED: anomalies are sparse (35/259 subruns, 42 entries), dominated by nPulses_median, while the trigger rate remains near 4.88 Hz and LVDS pins show no persistent zero, missing, high, or low behavior. INFERRED: this is more consistent with a transient DAQ/processing disturbance than a persistent PMT-base, LVDS, or trigger-rate fault. Run 1604 is the closest partial analogy, including channel 0 at z=45.8 and similar sparse nPulses anomalies; runs 1500 and 1605 provide weaker DAQ/CPU analogies. No exact historical match exists. UNKNOWN: restart history, CPU load, board matching, synchronization, queue occupancy, digitizer-rate consistency, and within-run configuration changes; these telemetry checks are needed to distinguish auto-restart, CPU saturation, and other DAQ transients.\"}"
